### What is type erasure?

Type erasure is the process by which the Java compiler removes all generic type information after performing compile-time type checking. The resulting bytecode contains no traces of type parameters. This was a deliberate design choice for backward compatibility with pre-Java-5 code.

```java
package com.bank.demo;

import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

public class TypeErasureDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<String> accounts = new ArrayList<>();
        List<Integer> amounts = new ArrayList<>();

        // At runtime, both are the same class
        System.out.println("accounts class: " + accounts.getClass().getName());
        System.out.println("amounts class: " + amounts.getClass().getName());
        System.out.println("Same class: " + (accounts.getClass() == amounts.getClass()));
    }
}
```

```text
accounts class: java.util.ArrayList
amounts class: java.util.ArrayList
Same class: true
```

### How does the compiler erase type parameters?

The compiler replaces each type parameter with its bound. If the type parameter is unbounded (`<T>`), it becomes Object. If it has a bound (`<T extends Number>`), it becomes Number. The compiler also inserts casts at call sites.

```java
// SOURCE CODE:
public class AccountBox<T> {
    private T account;
    public T getAccount() { return account; }
    public void setAccount(T account) { this.account = account; }
}

// AFTER ERASURE (bytecode equivalent):
public class AccountBox {
    private Object account;
    public Object getAccount() { return account; }
    public void setAccount(Object account) { this.account = account; }
}

// SOURCE CODE with bound:
public class NumberBox<T extends Number> {
    private T value;
    public T getValue() { return value; }
}

// AFTER ERASURE:
public class NumberBox {
    private Number value;   // T replaced with Number (the bound)
    public Number getValue() { return value; }
}
```

At the call site, the compiler inserts a cast:

```java
// Source:
AccountBox<String> box = new AccountBox<>();
String name = box.getAccount();

// Bytecode equivalent:
AccountBox box = new AccountBox();
String name = (String) box.getAccount(); // compiler-inserted cast
```

### What can you NOT do because of type erasure?

Type erasure creates several restrictions because the JVM has no knowledge of generic types at runtime.

```java
package com.bank.demo;

import java.util.List;

public class ErasureRestrictionsDemo {

    // RESTRICTION 1: Cannot instantiate T
    static <T> T createInstance() {
        // return new T(); // COMPILE ERROR: cannot instantiate type parameter
        return null;
    }

    // WORKAROUND: pass Class<T>
    static <T> T createInstance(Class<T> clazz) throws Exception {
        return clazz.getDeclaredConstructor().newInstance();
    }

    // RESTRICTION 2: Cannot use instanceof with parameterised type
    static void checkType(Object obj) {
        // if (obj instanceof List<String>) {} // COMPILE ERROR
        if (obj instanceof List<?>) { // OK: unbounded wildcard
            System.out.println("It is a List of something");
        }
    }

    // RESTRICTION 3: Cannot create generic array
    static <T> void createArray() {
        // T[] arr = new T[10]; // COMPILE ERROR
        // List<String>[] arr = new List<String>[10]; // COMPILE ERROR
    }

    // RESTRICTION 4: Cannot create generic exception
    // class BankException<T> extends Exception {} // COMPILE ERROR

    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        // Workaround for new T()
        String instance = createInstance(String.class);
        System.out.println("Created: '" + instance + "'");

        checkType(List.of("SAV2001"));
    }
}
```

```text
Created: ''
It is a List of something
```

### What are bridge methods?

Bridge methods are synthetic methods the compiler generates to maintain polymorphism after erasure. When a class implements a generic interface and narrows the type parameter, the erased interface method has a different signature than the class method. The compiler generates a bridge that delegates.

```java
package com.bank.demo;

import java.lang.reflect.Method;
import java.util.Arrays;

public class BridgeMethodDemo {

    interface Comparable<T> {
        int compareTo(T o);
    }

    // After erasure: interface Comparable { int compareTo(Object o); }

    static class Account implements Comparable<Account> {
        String id;
        Account(String id) { this.id = id; }

        @Override
        public int compareTo(Account other) {
            return this.id.compareTo(other.id);
        }
        // Compiler generates bridge method:
        // public int compareTo(Object o) { return compareTo((Account) o); }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Show that the bridge method exists via reflection
        Method[] methods = Account.class.getDeclaredMethods();
        for (Method m : methods) {
            if (m.getName().equals("compareTo")) {
                System.out.println(m + " | bridge: " + m.isBridge());
            }
        }

        Account a = new Account("SAV2001");
        Account b = new Account("CUR3001");
        System.out.println("compareTo: " + a.compareTo(b));
    }
}
```

```text
public int BridgeMethodDemo$Account.compareTo(BridgeMethodDemo$Account) | bridge: false
public int BridgeMethodDemo$Account.compareTo(java.lang.Object) | bridge: true
compareTo: 1
```

### How is this different from C# reified generics?

| Feature | Java (erased) | C# (reified) |
|---|---|---|
| Type info at runtime | Not available | Available |
| new T() | Not possible | Possible with `new()` constraint |
| typeof(List<string>) | Not possible | Possible |
| Runtime type check | instanceof List<?> only | typeof(List<string>) works |
| Backward compat | Seamless with raw types | Required new runtime |

### What are common mistakes?

#### Mistake: Trying to create instances of T

```java
// WRONG: T is erased at runtime
public class Repository<T> {
    public T createEmpty() {
        return new T(); // COMPILE ERROR
    }
}
```

```java
// CORRECT: pass Class<T> and use reflection
public class Repository<T> {
    private final Class<T> entityClass;
    public Repository(Class<T> entityClass) { this.entityClass = entityClass; }
    public T createEmpty() throws Exception {
        return entityClass.getDeclaredConstructor().newInstance();
    }
}
```

#### Mistake: Using instanceof with parameterised types

```java
// WRONG
if (obj instanceof List<String>) { } // COMPILE ERROR
```

```java
// CORRECT: use unbounded wildcard
if (obj instanceof List<?> list) { // pattern matching (Java 16+)
    System.out.println("List of size: " + list.size());
}
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

- **Reifiable vs non-reifiable types.** A reifiable type is one whose type information is fully available at runtime (String, List<?>, int[]). A non-reifiable type has some information erased (List<String>, Map<K,V>). Arrays require reifiable component types, which is why `new List<String>[10]` is illegal.
- **Super type tokens.** Frameworks like Jackson and Gson use `TypeReference<List<Account>>` or `TypeToken` to capture generic type information via anonymous subclasses, working around erasure.
- **Valhalla project.** Project Valhalla aims to bring specialised generics to Java, where `List<int>` would store primitives directly without boxing, partially addressing erasure limitations.

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- "Why did Java choose type erasure?" : For backward compatibility with pre-Java-5 code. Existing bytecode and libraries continued to work without changes.
- "Can you determine the generic type at runtime?" : Not directly. You can use super type tokens (anonymous subclass trick) or pass Class<T> explicitly.
- "What is a bridge method?" : A synthetic method generated by the compiler to bridge the erased signature and the specific signature, preserving polymorphism.
- "Why can you not create a generic array?" : Arrays are reified (know their component type at runtime) and throw ArrayStoreException on type violations. Generic arrays would break this guarantee because the type is erased.
